 and overdamped forward diffusion-based samplers share the same density evolution. This connection allows us to leverage classical analytical tools from nonequilibrium statistical physics, where the forward diffusion process has been thoroughly studied. We then apply two standard analytical tools developed therein, spectral analysis [ 33 , 34 , 35 ] and mean first-passage time (MFPT) analysis [ 36 , 37 ] , to characterize mixing behavior and demonstrate how small spectral gaps lead to practically slow convergence. Our work brings classical analytical tools from nonequilibrium statistical physics into the context of modern sampling methods, providing a complementary perspective on their practical limitations. Our claim is not a universal impossibility result for multimodal sampling, nor do we claim that WGF- or forward diffusion-based methods fail for every multimodal target. Instead, our critique concerns applying these methods, which rely solely on local, gradient-driven dynamics, to well-separated multimodal target densities. As shown below, in the metastable regime, these dynamics can require exponentially long times to transport probability mass between modes, leading to impractically slow convergence to the true target distributions. We emphasize that the contribution of this position paper is not a new sampling method, benchmark, or theory of metastability. Rather, it is a critical synthesis that connects the marginal-density equivalence between WGF and forward diffusion with classical spectral and MFPT analyses, thereby making explicit that these samplers inherit the metastability and slow inter-mode transport long understood for diffusion processes. We next provide minimal background on these two families of methods, together with pointers to more comprehensive references.

